// Arithmetic between two DIFFERENT varyings (t_e89cd261).
//
// An NV40 fragment instruction carries ONE input-source selector, so two
// operands of register type INPUT read the SAME varying whatever the
// emitter meant.  Both paths emitted this as a single ADD and it became
// `b - b`: zero, everywhere, silently - and the container's input mask
// still named TEXCOORD0, so nothing that trusted the mask could see it.
//
//     reference  MOVR R1, f[TEX2]; MOVR R0, f[TEX0]; ADDR R0, -R1, R0
//     ours       ADDR R0, f[TEX2], -f[TEX2]
//
// One operand has to be copied into a temp first.  The test does not
// assert a shape: it decodes every instruction and requires that none of
// them names two input registers, which is the hardware rule itself and
// holds for any shader, not just this one.
void main(float4 a : TEXCOORD0,
          float4 b : TEXCOORD2,
          out float4 oColor : COLOR)
{
    oColor = a - b;
}
